<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Volhynia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Volhynia is a historical region in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. It formed part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> until 1569. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a>, Polish negotiators secured the transfer of Volhynia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podolia">Podolia</a> from Lithuanian to Polish administration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/volhynia.jpg" alt="Volhynia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Сергій Венцеславський · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%92%27%D1%97%D0%B7%D0%BD%D0%B0_(%D0%9D%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%B1%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%BD%D0%B0)_%D0%B1%D0%B0%D1%88%D1%82%D0%B0_%D0%B7%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%BA%D1%83_%D0%9B%D1%83%D1%86%D1%8C%D0%BA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Large raids, often launched in winter when rivers were frozen, followed steppe routes such as the Muravsky Trail into Muscovy or crossed into Podolia and <b>Volhynia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Lithuanian tribes in the forests between the Neman and the Neris remained pagan long after their neighbours converted, organised under dukes whose number is suggested by a treaty of 1219 with Galicia–<b>Volhynia</b> that names twenty-one of them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…coveted prize, but it was contested by several branches of the dynasty and lost its primacy as other principalities grew stronger: Chernigov, Smolensk, Galicia and <b>Volhynia</b> in the south and west, Vladimir-Suzdal in the north-east, and the Novgorod Republic, which from 1136 chose and dismissed its own princes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>This gave the Polish nobility the leverage to insist on terms favourable to the Crown, including the transfer of Podlachia, <b>Volhynia</b> and the vast Ukrainian palatinates of Kiev and Bracław from Lithuanian to Polish administration.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Decembrists were liberal military and political dissidents, many of them nobles and guard officers; the Southern Society was dominated by army officers and landowners from Left-bank and Southern Ukraine, Sloboda Ukraine and <b>Volhynia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/decembrist-revolt">Decembrist revolt · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Yaroslav the Wise assigned his sons principalities in order of seniority: Kiev and Novgorod to the eldest, Iziaslav; Chernigov and Tmutarakan to Sviatoslav Yaroslavich; Pereyaslavl and Rostov-Suzdal to Vsevolod; Smolensk to Vyacheslav and <b>Volhynia</b> to Igor.</blockquote><a href="/societies/kievan-rus">Social Hierarchy of Kievan Rus'</a></li></ul></section>
